WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.738
Picture our restaurant moving into a food hall.

2
00:00:05.738 --> 00:00:08.324
Five kitchens now share one street door.

3
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:11.419
Guests shouldn't need to know which kitchen is where.

4
00:00:11.419 --> 00:00:14.904
So one host at the entrance sends each guest to the right place.

5
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:21.829
On test nights, the host sends one table in ten to the new tasting menu.

6
00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:23.416
We'll come back to that.

7
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:25.039
So here's the question.

8
00:00:25.039 --> 00:00:27.850
Our app already has a Service with an address.

9
00:00:27.850 --> 00:00:30.416
Why need anything more to let visitors in?

10
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:38.756
So far, you've used side doors: a NodePort on localhost 8081, or kubectl port-forward.

11
00:00:38.756 --> 00:00:40.243
Fine on a laptop.

12
00:00:40.243 --> 00:00:42.517
Not how real users arrive.

13
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:54.803
Real visitors need one door for many apps, routing by host name and path, TLS, the encryption behind https, in one place, and gradual releases.

14
00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:59.042
A Service of type LoadBalancer does give you an address.

15
00:00:59.042 --> 00:01:00.859
But it's a door with no host.

16
00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:03.525
It works with connections, at layer 4.

17
00:01:03.525 --> 00:01:10.317
It never opens the envelope: not the web address, not a header, the labelled extra lines in each request.

18
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:11.799
Excellent hinges.

19
00:01:11.799 --> 00:01:12.692
No opinions.

20
00:01:12.692 --> 00:01:17.529
We want a host, who opens each request and decides where it goes.

21
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:20.555
A program that does that is a proxy.

22
00:01:20.555 --> 00:01:25.713
It sits in front of your apps and passes each request on, reading it at layer 7.

23
00:01:25.933 --> 00:01:28.211
A host needs a book of rules.

24
00:01:28.211 --> 00:01:32.688
Kubernetes' older one is Ingress: host names, paths, TLS.

25
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:37.707
Everything else went into margin notes, each controller with its own shorthand.

26
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:48.052
Today the Ingress API is frozen, and ingress-nginx, the most popular controller, was retired on March 24, 2026.

27
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.724
The newer set of objects is the Gateway API.

28
00:01:51.724 --> 00:01:55.756
It splits the job by who owns what, just like the food hall.

29
00:01:55.967 --> 00:01:59.102
A GatewayClass says which controller handles the door.

30
00:01:59.102 --> 00:02:01.657
That's the company that staffs the entrance.

31
00:02:01.657 --> 00:02:04.328
The platform team creates it once per cluster.

32
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:10.577
A Gateway is the street door itself, with listeners, such as HTTP on port 80.

33
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:17.397
And an HTTPRoute is each kitchen's line in the host's book: slash goes to the hello-devops Service.

34
00:02:17.397 --> 00:02:20.861
App teams write these, and attach them to a Gateway.

35
00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:22.025
Let's try it.

36
00:02:22.025 --> 00:02:25.415
A fresh kind cluster, nothing extra installed.

37
00:02:25.415 --> 00:02:28.069
We apply a Gateway and an HTTPRoute.

38
00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:31.312
Pause and guess: does traffic start flowing?

39
00:02:33.533 --> 00:02:34.198
Well...

40
00:02:34.198 --> 00:02:35.623
not even close.

41
00:02:35.623 --> 00:02:37.143
The apply fails.

42
00:02:37.143 --> 00:02:39.043
No matches for kind.

43
00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:42.641
The Gateway API isn't built into Kubernetes.

44
00:02:42.641 --> 00:02:47.856
You install it as CRDs, new object types that the API server learns.

45
00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.814
Even then, nothing routes.

46
00:02:49.814 --> 00:02:51.964
A job description is not a host.

47
00:02:51.964 --> 00:02:55.660
A controller has to read these objects and act on them.

48
00:02:56.267 --> 00:03:01.046
The lab uses Envoy Gateway, a controller that runs the Envoy proxy.

49
00:03:01.046 --> 00:03:03.043
Watch what happens on apply.

50
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:03.568
One.

51
00:03:03.568 --> 00:03:06.134
The class eg names the controller.

52
00:03:06.134 --> 00:03:10.284
Envoy Gateway sees its own name, and accepts the class.

53
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:10.806
Two.

54
00:03:10.806 --> 00:03:14.019
The Gateway, hello-gateway, uses class eg.

55
00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:19.757
So the controller starts an Envoy proxy for it, a Deployment and a Service.

56
00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:20.424
Three.

57
00:03:20.424 --> 00:03:27.511
The HTTPRoute hello-devops attaches to hello-gateway, and points at the hello-devops Service.

58
00:03:27.733 --> 00:03:28.067
Four.

59
00:03:28.067 --> 00:03:33.403
The controller turns the route into Envoy settings, and sends them to the proxy.

60
00:03:33.403 --> 00:03:35.071
Now it starts forwarding.

61
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.294
Now a request.

62
00:03:36.294 --> 00:03:38.920
From curl, to Envoy, to hello-devops.

63
00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.978
Look where the controller is.

64
00:03:40.978 --> 00:03:43.391
It never handles a single request.

65
00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.073
It watches objects and writes settings.

66
00:03:46.073 --> 00:03:47.974
The proxy carries the traffic.

67
00:03:47.974 --> 00:03:50.320
The staffing company trains the host.

68
00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:52.032
The host greets the guests.

69
00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:54.994
Now, the tasting menu.

70
00:03:54.994 --> 00:03:59.243
A second version of the app, hello-canary, gets its own Service.

71
00:03:59.467 --> 00:04:03.259
A rule in an HTTPRoute can split traffic by weight.

72
00:04:03.259 --> 00:04:09.059
Ninety and ten: about nine requests in ten to hello-devops, one to the canary.

73
00:04:09.267 --> 00:04:10.895
We send fifty requests.

74
00:04:10.895 --> 00:04:14.507
Pause and guess: is it exactly forty-five and five?

75
00:04:16.733 --> 00:04:17.418
Let's see.

76
00:04:17.418 --> 00:04:22.827
Envoy picks a backend for each request at random, in proportion to the weights.

77
00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.304
This run: forty-four and six.

78
00:04:25.304 --> 00:04:28.224
Close to ninety, ten, but rarely exact.

79
00:04:28.433 --> 00:04:35.190
That's a canary release: a new version gets a small share of real traffic while you watch it.

80
00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:35.553
Good?

81
00:04:35.553 --> 00:04:36.788
Move the weights.

82
00:04:36.788 --> 00:04:37.078
Bad?

83
00:04:37.078 --> 00:04:38.749
Set the canary to zero.

84
00:04:38.967 --> 00:04:43.395
The lab adds a rule for testers: the header x-canary, always.

85
00:04:43.395 --> 00:04:45.719
That request matches both rules.

86
00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:46.517
Which wins?

87
00:04:47.733 --> 00:04:49.318
The most specific match.

88
00:04:49.318 --> 00:04:54.467
With equal paths, more header matches win, so testers always reach the canary.

89
00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:56.010
Two habits.

90
00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.772
First, set TLS once, on the Gateway.

91
00:04:58.772 --> 00:05:05.981
An HTTPS listener with mode Terminate decrypts at the proxy, with a certificate from a Secret.

92
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:09.035
The last hop, to your Pods, stays plain.

93
00:05:09.035 --> 00:05:10.736
Part 15 deals with that.

94
00:05:10.967 --> 00:05:15.255
Second, on a 404 or 500, don't touch the Gateway first.

95
00:05:15.255 --> 00:05:20.324
Usually the route never attached, or points at a missing Service.

96
00:05:20.324 --> 00:05:22.741
Run kubectl describe httproute.

97
00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:27.803
The moral of the story: a Service is a door, and a proxy is the host.

98
00:05:27.803 --> 00:05:33.084
The class staffs the door, the Gateway is the door, and each app writes its own route.

99
00:05:33.084 --> 00:05:36.707
The controller writes settings; the proxy carries requests.

100
00:05:37.533 --> 00:05:38.687
One quick check.

101
00:05:38.687 --> 00:05:46.544
A shop wants slash api sent to the Service api, and every other path to the Service web, through one Gateway.

102
00:05:46.544 --> 00:05:47.841
What do you write?

103
00:05:47.841 --> 00:05:48.995
Pause and think.

104
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.863
One HTTPRoute with two path rules.

105
00:05:53.863 --> 00:05:58.955
Slash api is more specific, so it wins, and slash takes the rest.

106
00:05:58.955 --> 00:06:00.521
Thanks for watching.
